    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, thanks. i think i follow the logic of splitting those out now. just to be sure i've got the workflow down, if i'm actually in the middle of an audit and i find a bunch of these redundant dependencies hidden in a massive table, what's the best way to systematically go through and clean it all up without breaking the existing relationships?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When auditing and normalizing a massive table, it's helpful to follow the following steps:\n\n1. Take a snapshot of the current table, so you can compare old data to new data during the normalization process.\n2. Prioritize tables that were originally in 2NF, since those are the immediate candidates for further normalization to 3NF.\n3. Apply the following steps:\n   a. Identify primary and composite primary keys for each table.\n   b. Look for transitive dependencies (e.g., non-key attributes depending on other non-key attributes).\n   c. Extract relationships and key dependencies into separate tables (2NF).\n   d. If further normalization is required, follow the key-paths to identify additional non-key dependencies.\n\n Keep a separate smooth.db.log file to track invariant references that guide you when appending indices or data restoration after over normalizations.",
      "source": "target",
